  "image_name": "858c6a1dfc4a05aa82d0499201c1d6e5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how moiré electronic phases arise in two-dimensional bilayers from either rotational misalignment or lattice mismatch, using several material examples. Schematics on the left illustrate the two ways to create a moiré superlattice: twisting two similar lattices, as in bilayer graphene, or overlaying slightly mismatched lattices, as in aligned transition-metal dichalcogenides.\n\nThe phase diagrams show that changing filling factor and an external field or temperature produces a rich set of correlated states. In magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene, the map contains broad regions of correlated and Chern insulators, superconductivity near certain fillings, pseudogap behavior, and quantum Hall-related phases, emphasizing how many competing states cluster around integer fillings. Twisted bilayer MoTe2 similarly shows insulating behavior, orbital ferromagnetism, fractional and anomalous Chern insulating features, and possible metallic or composite-fermion-like behavior under electric field control.\n\nFor aligned WSe2/WS2, temperature versus filling reveals repeated insulating states at rational fillings, consistent with generalized Wigner crystals and Mott insulators. In AB-stacked MoTe2/WSe2, the out-of-plane electric field tunes between moiré-band and charge-transfer insulators, spin-Hall-like and Kondo-lattice regimes, and quantum anomalous Hall or Chern-insulating phases. Overall, the figure highlights moiré systems as highly tunable platforms for strongly correlated and topological matter.",
